
{"id":"60e51f68-a03d-4059-97db-62091947b300","object":"chat.completion","created":1785016132,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    nodes_by_id = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        node_id = node['id']\\n        if not isinstance(node_id, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node id must be string\\\")\\n        if not all(k in node for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required field\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['removable'], bool):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"removable must be bool\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['depth'], int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"depth must be int\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['utility'], (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"utility must be numeric\\\")\\n        nodes_by_id[node_id] = node\\n        children_map.setdefault(node_id, [])\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(node_id)\\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] = symbol_counts.get(node['symbol'], 0) + 1\\n    node_ids = list(nodes_by_id.keys())\\n    node_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-nodes_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    removed_counts = {}\\n    for node_id in node_ids:\\n        node = nodes_by_id[node_id]\\n        if node['depth'] == 0 or node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        symbol = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            total = symbol_counts[symbol]\\n            removed_so_far = removed_counts.get(symbol, 0)\\n            if total - removed_so_far <= 1:\\n                continue\\n        children = children_map.get(node_id, [])\\n        if any(child not in removed_set for child in children):\\n            continue\\n        removed_set.add(node_id)\\n        removed_order.append(node_id)\\n        removed_counts[symbol] = removed_counts.get(symbol, 0) + 1\\n    retained_ids = sorted(nid for nid in nodes_by_id if nid not in removed_set)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The case payload has nodes (list of objects with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols (list of strings). The goal: grammar_guided_node_reduction, which likely means we need to remove some nodes (removable ones) while preserving required symbols and the tree structure? The description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\", \"interestingness oracle\", \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\". Given the task name SE-PE-01 and the provided fixtures, we need to determine which nodes to remove and which to retain.\n\nThe output must have removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids (both arrays of strings). We need to compute which nodes to remove based on some algorithm. The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So it seems we need to simulate a reduction process: we have a tree, and we try to remove nodes that are removable, but we must not remove the root (id \"root\"? likely root node's id is \"root\" and parent null) or nodes that have a symbol that is required, or nodes that have children that are retained. The interestingness oracle is that all required symbols must be present in the remaining tree. So we can remove a node if after removal, all required symbols are still present somewhere in the tree (i.e., not removed). Also we cannot remove a node if it has any retained children (presumably children that are not removed). We also must consider deeper nodes first, and for ties use node ID as stable tie break.\n\nWe need to decide the order of removal. The \"removed_node_ids\" should be in the order they were removed? The requirement: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be the order of removal, and retained_node_ids should be sorted (likely alphabetically?). Also retained_node_ids should include root and all nodes not removed.\n\nWe need to parse the tree structure from the nodes list. Each node has parent (id of parent or null for root). There is also depth field. We should build a tree (adjacency list). Then we need to try removing nodes that are removable (removable=true). But we must check constraints: cannot remove root (depth 0, parent null), cannot remove node whose symbol is in required_symbols, cannot remove node that has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed yet). So we need a process: start with all nodes as retained initially. Then iterate over nodes in order: deeper depth first, then by node ID string (stable tie break). For each node, check if it is removable, and not root, not required symbol, if all children are either not present (i.e., node has no children) or children are removed? Actually \"never remove a node with retained children\" means if the node has any child that is still retained, we cannot remove it. So we need to process bottom-up so that children are considered before parents. Since deeper depth first already does that. So we traverse nodes sorted by depth descending, then by node ID (string). For each node, if removable and not root and not required symbol and all children are already removed (i.e., marked removed) then tentatively remove it. But also need to check that after removal, all required symbols remain represented. That means we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node in the retained set (including possibly other nodes with that symbol) that is retained. So if the node we are considering to remove is the only node that provides a required symbol, we cannot remove it. So we need to check the current count of nodes with each required symbol among retained nodes after removal? Actually we should check before removal: the node's symbol may be required; if it is the only remaining node with that required symbol, removal would violate. But also if the node's symbol is not required, but its removal might affect other required symbols? Only the node's symbol matters; removal only removes that node, so only its symbol is lost. So we only need to check if the node's symbol is in required_symbols and the number of nodes of that symbol (including this node) among currently retained nodes is exactly 1, then we cannot remove because it would lose that required symbol. If there are multiple nodes with that symbol, we can remove one as long as at least one remains. Also if the node's symbol is not required, then there is no constraint from required symbols. However, the node may have children that are retained? But we already require that children must be removed first, so they are not retained. So we process bottom-up, so children are considered before parents. So when we consider a node, its children are already either removed or retained? Actually if children are retained, they would have been considered earlier? Since they are deeper, they would be processed before parent. But they might not be removable (e.g., required symbol or not removable). So they might remain. That would block removal of parent. So we must check if any child is still in the retained set. So we need to maintain a set of removed nodes. Initially empty. As we process each node in order, we check:\n\n- Node id is \"root\"? (depth==0 and parent==None? Actually root has id \"root\" in fixture, but we should check parent null). Cannot remove root.\n- removable==true\n- symbol not in required_symbols\n- all children of this node (by parent field) are either not in the nodes list? Actually children are those nodes whose parent equals this node's id. If there are no children, then condition satisfied. If there are children, they must all be already removed (i.e., in removed set). So we check: for each node that has parent==this node.id, we check if it is in removed set. If any child is not removed, cannot remove.\n- Also need to consider that after removal, required symbols still present: we need to ensure that if this node's symbol is required, there is at least one other node with that symbol that will remain (i.e., not removed). So we need to know total count of nodes with that symbol, and how many are already removed. Since we process in order, we can maintain a count of total nodes per symbol (from all nodes) and a count of removed nodes per symbol. Then check if (total[symbol] - removed[symbol] - 1) > 0 if symbol is required. Or simply check if there exists another node with that symbol that is not removed. Since we haven't removed this node yet, we can check: the number of nodes with that symbol that are not in removed set (excluding this node) must be >=1.\n\nIf all conditions satisfied, we remove the node: add to removed set, add to removed_order list, increment removed count for its symbol. Then continue.\n\nAfter processing all nodes, the retained nodes are all nodes not in removed set. Retained node IDs should be sorted (alphabetically? \"Return sorted retained IDs\" - likely sort lexicographically). Removed node IDs should be in order of removal.\n\nWe must be careful about nodes that are not removable (removable=false) - they cannot be removed regardless. Also root might have removable=false anyway.\n\nNow, the fixtures show nodes with depths 0,1,2. The required_symbols are