<= 1:  # only this node left\n            continue\n   # check children: all children must be in removed_set\n   children = children_map.get(node_id, [])\n   if any(child_id not in removed_set for child_id in children):\n        continue\n   # all conditions met: remove\n   removed_set.add(node_id)\n   removed_order.append(node_id)\n   removed_counts[node['symbol']] = removed_counts.get(node['symbol'], 0) + 1\n\n7. After loop, retained_ids = sorted(id for id in nodes_by_id if id not in removed_set)\n8. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nCheck: what about nodes that are removable but have children that are also removable? Since we process deeper first, children will be considered before parent. If a child is removable and conditions are met, it gets removed. Then parent may become removable if no retained children left. So the algorithm works. But what if a child is not removable (say required symbol) - then parent cannot be removed because that child is retained. That seems correct.\n\nAlso note: we must ensure that we don't remove a node that has a child that is still retained even if that child is not removable. So we only check children that exist. Good.\n\nNow, about \"removed_node_ids\" - \"actual removal order\". So we return the list in removal order.\n\nWe need to handle cases where the problem statement says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". But the fixtures are valid. However, we might implement validation: check that all nodes have required fields; if missing, raise ValueError. But specific checks? We can just implement and if something is missing, it will raise KeyError. But to match, we could wrap with try-except? The instruction says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So we should ensure that each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. And required_symbols is a list. Also check that parent references are valid (optional). But 