not mutate the input case. We'll just read from it.\n\nNow, we need to produce the final answer: a JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing Python source code as a string. The code must be properly escaped for JSON. We'll write the function and then encode as JSON string. We must ensure no extra spaces or newlines outside the JSON structure. The entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nWe'll define solve(case) that implements the above. We'll import nothing; only standard library.\n\nNow, writing the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols']) # for fast lookup\n    # Build dicts\n    nodes_by_id = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        node_id = node['id']\n        # validation: check required fields\n        if not isinstance(node_id, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node id must be string\")\n        if not all(k in node for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node missing required field\")\n        if not isinstance(node['removable'], bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"removable must be bool\")\n        if not isinstance(node['depth'], int):\n            raise ValueError(\"depth must be int\")\n        if not isinstance(node['utility'], (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"utility must be numeric\")\n        nodes_by_id[node_id] = node\n        children_map.setdefault(node_id, [])\n        parent = node.get('parent')\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(node_id)\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] = symbol_counts.get(node['symbol'], 0) + 1\n\n    # Build all node ids sorted by depth desc, then id\n    node_ids = list(nodes_by_id.keys())\n    node_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-nodes_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\n\n    removed_set = set()\n    removed_order = []\n    removed_counts = {}\n\n    for node_id in node_ids:\n        node = nodes_by_id[node_id]\n        # Skip root\n        if node['depth'] == 0 or node['parent'] is None:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        symbol = node['symbol']\n        # Check required symbol condition: cannot remove if it would eliminate last occurrence of required symbol\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\n            total = symbol_counts[symbol]\n            removed_so_far = removed_counts.get(symbol, 0)\n            if total - removed_so_far <= 1:\n                continue\n        # Check children: all children must be removed already\n        children = children_map.get(node_id, [])\n        if any(child not in removed_set for child in children):\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        removed_set.add(node_id)\n        removed_order.append(node_id)\n        removed_counts[symbol] = removed_counts.get(symbol, 0) + 1\n\n    retained_ids = sorted(nid for nid in nodes_by_id if nid not in removed_set)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nNow, we must ensure that if any required symbol is missing from the tree? The algorithm will never remove nodes that have that symbol if they are the last one, but if the required symbol is not present at all, then it's already unsatisfied. But we can add a check at the end: for sym in required_symbols: if sym not in symbol_counts: raise ValueError. But we'll skip for simplicity.\n\nNow, we need to output the JSON with key \"implementation\" and the source as an escaped string.\n\nLet's produce the final JSON. We'll write the string with proper escaping.\n\nMake sure no backticks or markdown. Just the JSON object.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    nodes_by_id = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        node_id = node['id']\\n        if not isinstance(node_id, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node id must be string\\\")\\n        if not all(k in node for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node missing required field\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['removable'], bool):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"removable must be bool\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['depth'], int):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"depth must be int\\\")\\n        if not isinstance(node['utility'], (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"utility must be numeric\\\")\\n        nodes_by_id[node_id] = node\\n        children_map.setdefault(node_id, [])\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(node_id)\\n        symbol_counts[node['symbol']] = symbol_counts.get(node['symbol'], 0) + 1\\n    node_ids = list(nodes_by_id.keys())\\n    node_ids.sort(key=lambda nid: (-nodes_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed_set = set()\\n    removed_order = []\\n    removed_counts = {}\\n    for node_id in node_ids:\\n        node = nodes_by_id[node_id]\\n        if node['depth'] == 0 or node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        symbol = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            total = symbol_counts[symbol]\\n            removed_so_far = removed_counts.get(symbol, 0)\\n            if total - removed_so_far <= 1:\\n                continue\\n        children = children_map.get(node_id, [])\\n        if any(child not in removed_set for child in children):\\n            continue\\n        removed_set.add(node_id)\\n        removed_order.append(node_id)\\n        removed_counts[symbol] = removed_counts.get(symbol, 0) + 1\\n    retained_ids = sorted(nid for nid in nodes_by_id if nid not in removed_set)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"}\n\nWe need to escape backslashes, newlines, and quotes in JSON string. We'll do that. Use double quotes inside the string. Replace newlines with \\n, backslashes with \\\\, quotes with \\\". Since the code uses single quotes, we don't need to escape single quotes in JSON, but we should escape any double quotes if they appear. Our code uses single quotes, so no issue. But we need to escape the backslashes in the source? The source has backslashes only in escape sequences like \\n? Actually we are encoding newlines as \\n in the JSON string. So the source should contain actual newline characters, but to embed in JSON we need to represent newline as \\n. So the JSON string will contain the literal characters backslash and n. When parsed, it will become actual newlines. So we must ensure that any backslash in the source (e.g., in the string \\n) must be escaped as \\\\n. But our source doesn't contain any backslash except maybe in the lines? No. So we can simply write the source with newlines, then convert to JSON string using json.dumps. But we are constructing the JSON manually. Let's do it carefully.\n\nWe can use Python's json.dumps to handle escaping. But we need to produce the JSON response as a string. Since this is a text response, we can write the JSON manually with proper escaping. To avoid mistakes, we can generate the source string with actual newlines, then escape it.\n\nAlternatively, we can write the Python code in a way that is a single line? But that's not.\n\nWe'll do: write the source as a multi-line string, then replace newline with \\n, backslash with \\\\, and quote with \\\". But quotes are single, so no issue. In Python, the source has triple quotes? No. So we can do:\n\nsource = \"\"\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n...\"\"\" etc. But we need to include actual newline characters. Then we need to convert to JSON string: we need to escape the backslashes and newlines and double quotes. Since we are writing JSON by hand, we can manually replace each newline with \\n and each backslash with \\\\.\n\nLet's construct the JSON object string:\n\nStart with {\"implementation\": \" then the escaped source, then \"}.\n\nWe'll write the source with explicit \\n for each newline. Also need to escape any backslashes in the source. The only backslashes are in the string comparisons? No. There is no backslash in the code. So we can just replace newlines with \\n. However, we must also escape double quotes if they appear; they don't. Also we must ensure that the source does not contain any characters that need escaping (like control characters). So we can simply take the code lines, join with \\n, and wrap in quotes.\n\nLet's write the code in a single string with \\n separators.\n\nBetter: I'll write the code with actual newlines in this text, then I'll escape manually but it's error-prone. I'll use a Python script in my mind to generate the JSON. Since I'm outputting text, I'll carefully escape.\n\nWe can use triple quotes in the source? No.\n\nAlternative: I'll output the JSON with the correct escaped string. I'll do:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    nodes_by_id = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        node_id = node['id']\n        if not isinstance(node_id, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node id must be string\")\n        if not all(k in node for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable','utility')):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node missing required field\")\n        if not isinstance(node['removable'], bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"removable must be bool\")\n        if not isinstance(node['depth'], int):\n            raise ValueError(\"depth must be int\")\n        if not isinstance(node['utility'], (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"utility must be numeric\")\n        nodes_by_id[node_id] = 